      "side": 1,
      "message": "Nit (optional): Unlike `RGBColorTableRow_NEON` (which preserves byte 3), `ARGBColorTableRow_NEON` overwrites all 4 bytes of each pixel, so you could pack `%w2`–`%w5` into a single 32-bit register and issue one post-indexed `str %w3, [%0], #4` instead of `strh` + `strb` + `strb` + `add`.",
      "revId": "1b1216abb649754576866b54f03a5229a02d9e52",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "337fea1f_c5575252",
        "filename": "source/row_neon64.cc",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 5927,
      "author": {
        "id": 1115898
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-27T22:52:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We benchmarked both on Apple M6: packing all 4 bytes with 3 bfi instructions and a single post-indexed str %w5, [%0], #4 took 8.24 ms (vs 8.17 ms for C) due to the serial bfi dependency chain waiting on all 4 table loads, whereas strh + strb + strb + add took 7.43 ms (1.10x faster than C) since each store depends on at most one ALU op after its load.",
      "parentUuid": "f2aed825_a3572d3a",
      "revId": "1b1216abb649754576866b54f03a5229a02d9e52",
